cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Responsible AI in Research and Research Training: A Global Capability Framework for University Research Leadership"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: report
authors:
  - family-names: "Zyphur"
    given-names: "Michael J."
    affiliation: "Instats"
    email: "support@instats.org"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3237-7892"
repository-code: "https://github.com/mzyphur/responsible-ai-in-research-training"
url: "https://instats.org/publications/responsible-ai-in-research-training/"
abstract: >-
  A global capability framework for senior university research
  leadership on responsible AI use in research and research training.
  Synthesises national research-funder policies (UKRI, NSF, ARC, DFG,
  ERC, NORDIS, A*STAR), institutional AI policies at top doctoral
  universities, and existing AI-literacy and researcher-development
  frameworks (UNESCO, OECD, JISC, EDUCAUSE, Vitae UK Researcher
  Development Framework, Council of Graduate Schools). Specifies what
  responsible AI use looks like in practice at PhD level: AI tools as
  search, co-author, validator, and tutor; privacy, plagiarism, and
  intellectual-property governance; reproducibility, hallucination,
  and adversarial-validation discipline; and the AI-literacy
  competencies humans need to keep research valid, replicable, and
  ethical when AI is in the loop. Benchmarks readiness at the system
  level and the organisation level, including whether universities
  have AI policies that go beyond plagiarism. Written as a global
  view that supports later regional adaptations.
